-- CA_0377: gun-policy Season 3 reasoning, text only — bills the 2026-11-12 rung 4 rulings moved.
--
-- The rulings of 2026-11-12 (annex docs/codebook/annex/season3/gun-policy.md, rung 4; PR #1406) took ten bills off
-- rung 4:
--   * out of scope — a capitol carry right for named officials: IN SB 43 (2017), the capitol part of IN HB 1084 (2024);
--   * direction only (shows a side, seats no one, makes no record two-sided) — paperwork venue or one narrow
--     situation: MI HB 4980, PA HB 1953, MI HB 5113, MI HB 4855 (all 2025), IN HB 1071 (2017), VA HB 540 (2026); and
--     emergency powers: MI HB 5187, MI SB 11 (2021).
-- PA HB 1747 (2019), fee cuts and waivers, training changes and carry in one more kind of place stay rung 4.
--
-- The 35 records whose seat turned on these bills were re-coded and approved the same day (batches gun-policy-in-04-r4,
-- -mi-02-r4, -pa-03-r4, -va-03-r4). This file fixes the text of the seats that did NOT move: 140 seated rows (34 at rung 3, 40 at rung 4, 66 at rung 5)
-- whose reasoning still called a moved bill a rung 4 act, "the carry-easier rung", a carry easing under furthest act,
-- or a permit-keeping easing read under the licence rule. Each clause now says what the ruling says.
--
-- Selection: the live reasoning of all 1,299 rows at revision 41349e39 was searched for the ten bills (206 matched).
-- 37 were the re-coded rows (already correct). Of the other 169, 28 needed no change (the bill is a No vote, a
-- different state's bill with the same number, or the text already reads it as direction only), and 1 was held back:
-- 085e88aa-edfd-435e-90bb-d84c5912efde (rung 4), whose seat rests only on own words about HB 1084's capitol carry, so
-- it needs a re-code, not a text fix.
--
-- Every new text was checked against its old text: no quoted span that is not copied exactly from the old text, no
-- bill not already named, no new annex label, no party name. Planted faults of each kind turned that check red first.
--
-- Reasoning text only. value, sources, evidence, review rows and outcomes are unchanged; the post-verify asserts every
-- value. Each row is guarded on its exact old text (kept below, so the overwritten value stays on record): the file is
-- idempotent and refuses a row someone else has since edited. STAGE5.md "Reasoning text is voter-facing".

CREATE TEMP TABLE _ca0377_fix (politician_id uuid PRIMARY KEY, value int NOT NULL, old_text text NOT NULL, new_text text NOT NULL);

INSERT INTO _ca0377_fix (politician_id, value, old_text, new_text) VALUES
  ('00509f90-abfc-4d6f-9d7b-8bc4610a97ca', 4,
   $r4$Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00" and HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, Yea on HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee" and HB 4030 (2021), the licence fee cut from "$100.00" to "not more than $45.00": each makes the carry permit easier to get, rung 4 under annex rule two. Yea on HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow" (rung 4) and co-sponsor of HB 4943 (2023), the Capitol Commission shall not "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying "in the State Capitol Building" (carry in a public building, rung 4). The permit stays in every bill. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128) and said in 2022 'I don't believe there need to be any additional restrictions on gun ownership', which shows a side only; no record repeals a permit, so the furthest act is rung 4: make carrying a gun easier. HB 4434 (2019) is a penalty change for a gun crime (adjacent). SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00", and Yea on HB 4030 (2021), the licence fee cut from "$100.00" to "not more than $45.00": each makes the carry permit easier to get, rung 4 under annex rule two. Co-sponsor of HB 4943 (2023), the Capitol Commission shall not "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying "in the State Capitol Building" (carry in a public building, rung 4). The permit stays in every bill. He also co-sponsored and voted Yea on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, and voted Yea on HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow". Since the 2026-11-12 ruling these three show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128) and said in 2022 'I don't believe there need to be any additional restrictions on gun ownership', which shows a side only; no record repeals a permit, so the furthest act is rung 4: make carrying a gun easier. HB 4434 (2019) is a penalty change for a gun crime (adjacent). SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$),
  ('01a7df6b-b5ab-434d-9ce8-2e4c4a0e62d7', 5,
   $r4$His own words, quoted on the 2026 introduction of the bill package to end the permit to carry a concealed pistol (HB 5653-5657): "These permit requirements are a misguided attempt to protect people. In reality, they only serve as a hindrance to responsible gun owners" and "It's time for Michigan to put into law what dozens of other states" ... "have already realized." That meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit' … 'such as the permit to carry' (in-term, 2026-03-06). The package has no instrument in the store, so his sponsorship of it is not used. His records agree and do not conflict: Yeas on HB 4284, HB 4980, HB 5113, HB 4855 (rung 4 acts that keep the permit) and Nays on the 2023 restriction bills.$r4$,
   $r4$His own words, quoted on the 2026 introduction of the bill package to end the permit to carry a concealed pistol (HB 5653-5657): "These permit requirements are a misguided attempt to protect people. In reality, they only serve as a hindrance to responsible gun owners" and "It's time for Michigan to put into law what dozens of other states" ... "have already realized." That meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit' … 'such as the permit to carry' (in-term, 2026-03-06). The package has no instrument in the store, so his sponsorship of it is not used. His records agree and do not conflict: Yeas on HB 4284 (a rung 4 act that keeps the permit) and on HB 4980, HB 5113 and HB 4855 (which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts) and Nays on the 2023 restriction bills.$r4$),
  ('02cbc0b6-0f8f-4af0-a698-7b95e4757750', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She also co-sponsored HB 4127 and HB 4128. She voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (direction only; a No does not cancel a Yea).$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Her Yea on HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27), keeps the permit and since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on. It is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She also co-sponsored HB 4127 and HB 4128. She voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (direction only; a No does not cancel a Yea).$r4$),
  ('0400359e-2de8-40c5-8405-4476bddf86dd', 4,
   $r4$Every counted loosening act keeps the permit and makes carrying easier: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (carry allowed where it was barred, permit system kept: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (applying at a nearer office, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (an easier permit, a fee waiver, annex rule two: rung 4); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). His No votes on the 2023 restrictions show a direction only; HB 4481 (vote and sponsorship) is gated. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$,
   $r4$Every counted loosening act keeps the permit and makes carrying easier: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). Three more loosening bills since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts: HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee". His No votes on the 2023 restrictions show a direction only; HB 4481 (vote and sponsorship) is gated. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$),
  ('0444c4ff-1fde-4be2-92d4-81f843ab8499', 4,
   $r4$Yea on single-subject bills: HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00"; HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00"; HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; HB 4855 (2025) says the act does not prohibit "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"; HB 5113 (2025) lets an applicant whose licence was refused for an application error "submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee"; HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; and SB 11 (2021), which keeps a clerk's duty to "Issue a concealed pistol license" in an emergency. These keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). He voted No on the rung 3 checks, red-flag and place-ban bills (direction only). His Yea on SB 79 (safe storage) is adjacent, not a rung 3 act, and his sponsored HB 4003/HB 4434 only soften the penalty for an expired licence (direction only); SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act. His quoted words (Those are dead on arrival) name no rung.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on single-subject bills: HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00"; HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00". These keep the concealed pistol licence and make the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His Yeas on five more bills since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts: HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; HB 4855 (2025) says the act does not prohibit "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"; HB 5113 (2025) lets an applicant whose licence was refused for an application error "submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee"; HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; and SB 11 (2021) keeps a clerk's duty to "Issue a concealed pistol license" in an emergency. He voted No on the rung 3 checks, red-flag and place-ban bills (direction only). His Yea on SB 79 (safe storage) is adjacent, not a rung 3 act, and his sponsored HB 4003/HB 4434 only soften the penalty for an expired licence (direction only); SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act. His quoted words (Those are dead on arrival) name no rung.$r4$),
  ('066daefa-0c25-447b-8a80-e4007ee3103d', 5,
   $r4$Yea on IN HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, conference report 68-30 House / 30-20 Senate: it repeals the licence to carry (the text cites "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and repeals IC 35-47-2-2), replacing it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; that list is the permit-free carry rule's own eligibility line, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3): rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Her own words (2022-01-11 release on HB 1077, permit-free carry) back the same act: words to the same effect (no readable page prints them) Yeas on HB 1084 (2024), HB 1071 (2017) and SB 43 (2017) are rung 4 acts on the same side. Her line that the bill keeps the federal buyer check describes current law and states no rung 3 act. Her HB 1296 co-sponsorship is not used (off-version: the permit repeal was added in conference). Other gun findings are gated (near-unanimous or multi-subject) or out of scope (adjacent: shooting-range zoning SB 176, insurer gun-free-zone signs HB 1274, licence-holder privacy HB 1323, local suits against the industry HB 1235, police gun trades HB 1143); a vote on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. If HB 1365 (2023) were read as a rung 2 act (its machine-gun definition), this person's ungated Yea on it would sit beside the rung 5 act on the other side of the middle, and the code would change to doesnt-fit (two-sided).$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on IN HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, conference report 68-30 House / 30-20 Senate: it repeals the licence to carry (the text cites "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and repeals IC 35-47-2-2), replacing it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; that list is the permit-free carry rule's own eligibility line, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3): rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Her own words (2022-01-11 release on HB 1077, permit-free carry) back the same act: words to the same effect (no readable page prints them). Her Yeas on HB 1084 (2024), HB 1071 (2017) and SB 43 (2017) are not rung acts since the 2026-11-12 ruling: the capitol carry right for state officials in HB 1084 and SB 43 is out of scope, and HB 1071 (60 days of carry for protection-order holders) shows only which side she is on. None of them makes the record two-sided. Her line that the bill keeps the federal buyer check describes current law and states no rung 3 act. Her HB 1296 co-sponsorship is not used (off-version: the permit repeal was added in conference). Other gun findings are gated (near-unanimous or multi-subject) or out of scope (adjacent: shooting-range zoning SB 176, insurer gun-free-zone signs HB 1274, licence-holder privacy HB 1323, local suits against the industry HB 1235, police gun trades HB 1143); a vote on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. If HB 1365 (2023) were read as a rung 2 act (its machine-gun definition), this person's ungated Yea on it would sit beside the rung 5 act on the other side of the middle, and the code would change to doesnt-fit (two-sided).$r4$),
  ('0ddfaf89-b056-4909-97c2-c74d08ce508d', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She co-sponsored HB 4146. Her Nays on HB 4284, HB 4855 and HB 4980 show a side only; HB 4481 is gated; her campaign line on "gun safety legislation" names no rung.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), which keeps the permit and since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on. It is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She co-sponsored HB 4146. Her Nays on HB 4284, HB 4855 and HB 4980 show a side only; HB 4481 is gated; her campaign line on "gun safety legislation" names no rung.$r4$),
