   $r4$Yea in the Senate on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Senate Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) (rung 4) sit below it. His abstentions (HB 1137 2025, HB 1323 2023, HB 1558 2021, HB 1651 2019) are on near-unanimous votes and are gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea in the Senate on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, his Senate Yea on HB 1071 (2017) shows only which side he is on, and his Senate Yeas on SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024), capitol carry rights for state officials, are out of scope (not rung acts). His abstentions (HB 1137 2025, HB 1323 2023, HB 1558 2021, HB 1651 2019) are on near-unanimous votes and are gated.$r4$),
  ('263a3e60-33d9-40c8-8e2c-af8189584f31', 5,
   $r4$In his House term he voted Yea on MI HB 4416 (2017-06-07, 59-49, gate none, single-subject), which amends MCL 750.227 so the concealed-carry ban "does not apply to" … "(a) A firearm" and strikes the words "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law", keeping a carry ban only for "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm". That removes the permit to carry for adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (rung 5). The store links this 2017 roll call to him through the official 2017-18 House roll; it is his, in the same legislature (V5 in-term). His rung 4 Yeas (HB 5187 and SB 11, 2022; HB 4030, 2021) sit below it under furthest act; HB 4474 and SB 219 (2017) are gated. A rung 5 act conflicts with any rung 1-3 act, but he has none: as Senator he voted No on every rung 3 bill in the findings (SB 76, HB 4138, SB 83, HB 4146, HB 4127, HB 4128, SB 225, SB 226, SB 331), his Yeas on the bump-stock bans SB 942 and SB 224 are direction-only (an accessory is not a type of firearm, annex rule three), and SB 22 copies a federal ban. SB 181 has no instrument file; his 2023 words show a side only.$r4$,
   $r4$In his House term he voted Yea on MI HB 4416 (2017-06-07, 59-49, gate none, single-subject), which amends MCL 750.227 so the concealed-carry ban "does not apply to" … "(a) A firearm" and strikes the words "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law", keeping a carry ban only for "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm". That removes the permit to carry for adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (rung 5). The store links this 2017 roll call to him through the official 2017-18 House roll; it is his, in the same legislature (V5 in-term). His rung 4 Yea on HB 4030 (2021) sits below it under furthest act; his Yeas on HB 5187 and SB 11 (2022) since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts; HB 4474 and SB 219 (2017) are gated. A rung 5 act conflicts with any rung 1-3 act, but he has none: as Senator he voted No on every rung 3 bill in the findings (SB 76, HB 4138, SB 83, HB 4146, HB 4127, HB 4128, SB 225, SB 226, SB 331), his Yeas on the bump-stock bans SB 942 and SB 224 are direction-only (an accessory is not a type of firearm, annex rule three), and SB 22 copies a federal ban. SB 181 has no instrument file; his 2023 words show a side only.$r4$),
  ('26f334da-cd5d-45e6-967f-4df39389e1e8', 4,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of MI HB 4943 (2023): the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol; Yea on HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"), HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4855 (does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "(c) The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow") and SB 11. These widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. No rung 5 act; her Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only. HB 4434 (her prime sponsorship, penalty for an expired licence) is sentencing, adjacent. Her campaign-page line is third person and not used. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of MI HB 4943 (2023): the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol; Yea on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"). These widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. Her Yeas on HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4855 (does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "(c) The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow") and SB 11 since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side she is on and are not rung acts. No rung 5 act; her Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only. HB 4434 (her prime sponsorship, penalty for an expired licence) is sentencing, adjacent. Her campaign-page line is third person and not used. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$),
  ('27de0fdf-0563-4b96-8cfc-a8191ec946c8', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She was prime sponsor of HB 4146 and co-sponsored HB 4127 and HB 4128. Her HB 4003 Yea (a civil fine for some licence violations) is direction-only and makes nothing two-sided; her Nays on other carry bills show a side only; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"), which keeps the permit and since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on. It is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She was prime sponsor of HB 4146 and co-sponsored HB 4127 and HB 4128. Her HB 4003 Yea (a civil fine for some licence violations) is direction-only and makes nothing two-sided; her Nays on other carry bills show a side only; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated.$r4$),
  ('288171b4-e6cc-4e18-8552-6296a316cae2', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4030 (2021), CPL fee cut from $100.00 to "not more than $45.00"; HB 4284 (2025), CPL renewal fee cut from $115.00 to "$30.00"; HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 4980 (2025), apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27); HB 5187 (2021), an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm (61-40); SB 11 (2021), concealed pistol licences processed during a declared emergency. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). Her 2019 prime sponsorship of HB 4434 and Yea on a 2021 expired-licence penalty bill (a gun-crime penalty, a gun-crime penalty) are adjacent.$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4030 (2021), CPL fee cut from $100.00 to "not more than $45.00"; HB 4284 (2025), CPL renewal fee cut from $115.00 to "$30.00". Since the 2026-11-12 ruling these show only which side she is on and are not rung acts: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 4980 (2025), apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27); HB 5187 (2021), an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm (61-40); SB 11 (2021), concealed pistol licences processed during a declared emergency. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a fee cut that keeps the permit conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, and the direction-only acts conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). Her 2019 prime sponsorship of HB 4434 and Yea on a 2021 expired-licence penalty bill (a gun-crime penalty, a gun-crime penalty) are adjacent.$r4$),
  ('2a4695f6-047a-46bb-82f4-c7360b97f203', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 68-30, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His Yea on HB 1084 (2024) says "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", which widens where a gun may be carried: rung 4 (its registry and payment-code parts state no rung act) is a rung 4 act on the same side; furthest act → 5. His own words (2021, 2022) back permit-free carry ('Every responsible Hoosier should have the right to defend themselves'), but HB-1077-2022 and HB-1369-2021 have no instrument file and seat nothing; his HB-1296 co-sponsorship is the insurance bill as filed; his No on HB-1365-2023 is no rung act.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 68-30, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His Yea on HB 1084 (2024) says "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for state officials, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry and payment-code parts state no rung act. Furthest act → 5. His own words (2021, 2022) back permit-free carry ('Every responsible Hoosier should have the right to defend themselves'), but HB-1077-2022 and HB-1369-2021 have no instrument file and seat nothing; his HB-1296 co-sponsorship is the insurance bill as filed; his No on HB-1365-2023 is no rung act.$r4$),
  ('2cf979ee-d18c-446d-addd-7e2233b390c1', 4,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm" (lifting a carry limit in a declared emergency); prime sponsor of, and Yea on, HB 5113 (a carry-licence reapplication 'without paying an additional fee'); co-sponsor of HB 4943 (the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol); Yeas on HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}") and SB 11 (licences still issued in emergencies). All widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier' (annex rule two, annex the licence rule list). No rung 5 act; his Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
