   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling these show only which side he is on and are not rung acts: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27). These conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). He voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (a No shows a direction only and does not cancel a Yea). His 2023 words ("thoughts and prayers are not enough"; "common-sense steps") name no policy (rhetorical).$r4$),
  ('44215a1e-cb8f-41ae-8953-596868c36007', 4,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; co-sponsor of HB 4943 (the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol); Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"), HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4855 and SB 11. These widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. No rung 5 act; her Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only, and her 2022 words against a storage mandate are adjacent (storage). HB 4434 (penalty for an expired licence) is sentencing, adjacent. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; co-sponsor of HB 4943 (the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol); Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"), HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4855 and SB 11. HB 4943 and HB 4284 widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 5187, HB 4980, HB 4855 and SB 11 show only which side she is on and are not rung acts. No rung 5 act; her Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only, and her 2022 words against a storage mandate are adjacent (storage). HB 4434 (penalty for an expired licence) is sentencing, adjacent. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$),
  ('44808453-d7f5-48d9-abb9-6ce42ec7d1ed', 5,
   $r4$Yea on MI HB 4416 (2017 House, 59-49, single-subject), which removes the licence to carry a concealed pistol: Sec. 227(3) leaves only "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm shall not carry a pistol concealed", deleting "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law". That meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit' … 'such as the permit to carry'; it is a record from his House years, the same legislature as his Senate seat. His other acts are on the same side: Yeas on HB 5187, SB 11 and HB 4030 (rung 4 acts) and Nays on every Senate restriction bill (2023 checks and red-flag orders, polling-place and Capitol bans, serial numbers, bump stocks). HB 4474 and SB 219 (2017) are near-unanimous and gated; no own words found.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on MI HB 4416 (2017 House, 59-49, single-subject), which removes the licence to carry a concealed pistol: Sec. 227(3) leaves only "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm shall not carry a pistol concealed", deleting "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law". That meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit' … 'such as the permit to carry'; it is a record from his House years, the same legislature as his Senate seat. His other acts are on the same side: Yeas on HB 4030 (a rung 4 act) and on HB 5187 and SB 11 (since the 2026-11-12 ruling these show only which side he is on and are not rung acts) and Nays on every Senate restriction bill (2023 checks and red-flag orders, polling-place and Capitol bans, serial numbers, bump stocks). HB 4474 and SB 219 (2017) are near-unanimous and gated; no own words found.$r4$),
  ('45e48053-6db5-4e63-a5d7-2ad613340bd9', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of, and Yea on the final House vote (68-30) for, HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. His Yeas on HB 1084 (2024), HB 1071 and SB 43 (2017) are rung 4 acts on the same side. His final HB 1365 (2023) vote was No (concurrence). His 2020 words on waiving licence fees ("Hoosiers should not have to pay fees for a gun license") lean the same way.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of, and Yea on the final House vote (68-30) for, HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, his Yea on HB 1071 shows only which side he is on, and his Yeas on HB 1084 (2024) and SB 43 (2017), capitol carry rights for state officials, are out of scope (not rung acts). His final HB 1365 (2023) vote was No (concurrence). His 2020 words on waiving licence fees ("Hoosiers should not have to pay fees for a gun license") lean the same way.$r4$),
  ('462fbada-42af-4bf0-8562-cb618e8752d1', 4,
   $r4$In the House (same legislature, in-term) he voted Yea on three single-subject bills: HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00"; HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; and SB 11 (2021) keeps a clerk's duty to "Issue a concealed pistol license" during an emergency. These keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His Senate gun records are all No votes on restrictions (direction only); his Yea and prime sponsorship of SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act; his own words ("Firearms in the hands of criminals.") name no policy. No rung 1-3 or 5 act stands beside the rung 4 acts.$r4$,
   $r4$In the House (same legislature, in-term) he voted Yea on three single-subject bills: HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00"; HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; and SB 11 (2021) keeps a clerk's duty to "Issue a concealed pistol license" during an emergency. HB 4030 keeps the concealed pistol licence and makes the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 5187 and SB 11 show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. His Senate gun records are all No votes on restrictions (direction only); his Yea and prime sponsorship of SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act; his own words ("Firearms in the hands of criminals.") name no policy. No rung 1-3 or 5 act stands beside the rung 4 act.$r4$),
  ('47ceb291-6814-4358-be80-5415b076e993', 4,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of MI HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote on file): the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol; Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"), HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4855 (does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "(c) The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow") and HB 5113 (fee waiver on reapplying). These widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. No rung 5 act; her Nays on the 2023 restriction bills (and on SB 22) show a side only. Her voter-guide answer 'None' is undated and names no rung. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of MI HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote on file): the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol; Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"), HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4855 (does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "(c) The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow") and HB 5113 (fee waiver on reapplying). HB 4943 and HB 4284 widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 4980, HB 4855 and HB 5113 show only which side she is on and are not rung acts. No rung 5 act; her Nays on the 2023 restriction bills (and on SB 22) show a side only. Her voter-guide answer 'None' is undated and names no rung. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$),
  ('4803bc68-6764-4fe2-b38f-a2d17f50a458', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (direction only). Her co-sponsorship of HB 4481 (reciprocity website) is gated with its 92-9 vote.$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling these show only which side she is on and are not rung acts: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27). These conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (direction only). Her co-sponsorship of HB 4481 (reciprocity website) is gated with its 92-9 vote.$r4$),
  ('480ea00a-4cc4-4a4a-8117-607f7136c25e', 4,
   $r4$As State Representative she co-sponsored and voted Yea on MI HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13, gate none), allowing "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow", and voted Yea on HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45; apply to a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4284 (CPL renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00") and HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"). Each keeps the permit and widens carry or makes the permit easier: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4; annex rule two). No act repeals a permit and none is a rung 1-3 act. HB 4481 is gated (92-9); her prime-sponsored HB 4949 (shooting-range liability) is adjacent.$r4$,
   $r4$As State Representative she co-sponsored and voted Yea on MI HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13, gate none), allowing "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow", and voted Yea on HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45; apply to a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4284 (CPL renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00") and HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"). HB 4284 keeps the permit and makes it easier to get: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4; annex rule two). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 4855, HB 4980 and HB 5113 show only which side she is on and are not rung acts. No act repeals a permit and none is a rung 1-3 act. HB 4481 is gated (92-9); her prime-sponsored HB 4949 (shooting-range liability) is adjacent.$r4$),
  ('48f99b45-486d-4d6d-8f48-ed21a793778f', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 68-30, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His other ungated Yeas are rung 4 acts on the same side: HB 1071 (2017) lets protection-order holders carry without a licence for 60 days ("A person may carry a handgun without a license if the person" … "is protected by a protection order"), a carry widening for one group while the licence system stays: rung 4; HB 1084 (2024) says "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", which widens where a gun may be carried: rung 4 (its registry and payment-code parts state no rung act); SB 43 (2017) says a legislator "who: (1) possesses a valid Indiana license to carry a handgun" … "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", which widens where a gun may be carried while the licence system stays: rung 4. Furthest act → 5. His HB-1296 co-sponsorship is the insurance bill as filed (not a gun act); HB-1424-2018 (licence fee cut) has no instrument file and seats nothing; his gated Yeas (e.g. HB-1651-2019, 93-1) and Yeas on HB-1365-2023, SB-119-2019 and SB-344-2017 are no rung acts. His own words (2018, 2020) on cutting licence fees show the same side only.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 68-30, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, none of his other ungated Yeas is a rung act: HB 1071 (2017) lets protection-order holders carry without a licence for 60 days ("A person may carry a handgun without a license if the person" … "is protected by a protection order"), carry for one narrow situation while the licence system stays, which shows only which side he is on; HB 1084 (2024) says "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (its registry and payment-code parts state no rung act); SB 43 (2017) says a legislator "who: (1) possesses a valid Indiana license to carry a handgun" … "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", also a capitol carry right for named officials and out of scope. Furthest act → 5. His HB-1296 co-sponsorship is the insurance bill as filed (not a gun act); HB-1424-2018 (licence fee cut) has no instrument file and seats nothing; his gated Yeas (e.g. HB-1651-2019, 93-1) and Yeas on HB-1365-2023, SB-119-2019 and SB-344-2017 are no rung acts. His own words (2018, 2020) on cutting licence fees show the same side only.$r4$),
  ('4a199dea-8abf-4b9a-a780-f9036be31ab7', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 place ban HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 acts HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. Her Nays on HB 4284 and HB 4980 show a side only; HB 4481 is gated; HB 4949, HB 5450 and HB 6145/6146 are adjacent; her own words ("Gun violence in the home is preventable") name no rung act.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 place ban HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side she is on and are not rung acts. They conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 act, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. Her Nays on HB 4284 and HB 4980 show a side only; HB 4481 is gated; HB 4949, HB 5450 and HB 6145/6146 are adjacent; her own words ("Gun violence in the home is preventable") name no rung act.$r4$),
  ('4a80ac94-9af3-4313-bec5-a90b50a4e276', 5,
   $r4$Her own words, quoted by CBS Detroit on 2023-06-08 as she introduced a bill to let adults carry a handgun without a permit: "Constitutional carry empowers citizens to be able to defend themselves as they should" (constitutional carry = permitless carry; backing it states rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). Her record is on the same side and does not contradict it: Yea on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut), HB 4855 (bow-hunter carry), HB 4980 (any-county CPL application), HB 5113, co-sponsor of HB 4855, HB 4943 and HB 4980 (all rung 4), and No on every 2023 restriction (direction only); furthest act is rung 5. The statement is from her first term (2023), so code may send it to review as out of cycle; the bill she introduced is not in the store, so no record seats rung 5. The caucus bio line is third-person and supports nothing.$r4$,
