   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00", HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow" (since the 2026-11-12 ruling HB 4980, HB 5113 and HB 4855 show only which side she is on and are not rung acts), co-sponsor of HB 4943 (2023), the Capitol Commission shall not "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying "in the State Capitol Building". Her own words state the same act: 'Law-abiding gun owners should be allowed to carry on college campuses' (2023, on her campus-carry bill) and her capitol bill 'ensures state agencies cannot arbitrarily restrict concealed carry in the people's building' (2025): carry in places it was barred, by licence holders, rung 4. She voted No on the rung 3 bills; nothing repeals a permit. Furthest act: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier. Her own bills HB 4285, 4756, 5132 and 4831 have no instrument file in the store and are not used.$r4$),
  ('4dcf4fa8-90f3-4717-9fa6-8e2fdbe9e602', 5,
   $r4$Yea in the Senate on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Senate Yea on HB 1084 (2024) (rung 4) sits below it. His 2021 statement about the timing of a constituent email names no law and is not relied on; his authorship of HB 1084 is not cited because only the enrolled text is in the store.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea in the Senate on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Senate Yea on HB 1084 (2024), a capitol carry right for state officials, is out of scope since the 2026-11-12 ruling (not a rung act). His 2021 statement about the timing of a constituent email names no law and is not relied on; his authorship of HB 1084 is not cited because only the enrolled text is in the store.$r4$),
  ('4df9c587-c16b-4a88-8058-64b6ad5aaf67', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 acts HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146. His HB 4434 Yea and sponsorship (2019, a civil fine for some licence violations) are direction-only and make nothing two-sided; his Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated. His 2021 words on barring guns at the Capitol agree with a place ban.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. They conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146. His HB 4434 Yea and sponsorship (2019, a civil fine for some licence violations) are direction-only and make nothing two-sided; his Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated. His 2021 words on barring guns at the Capitol agree with a place ban.$r4$),
  ('4e30ee0d-4b36-4337-889e-7a26e1fff510', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor and Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (68-30, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried (the carry-easier rung); its registry ban ("shall not knowingly or willfully keep" … any list, record, or registry of privately owned firearms) repeals no permit or registration requirement (66-26) is on the same side (furthest act: 5). Under the orchestrator note (runner ruling pending), HB 1365 (2023, machine-gun definition widened to conversion parts) is treated as no rung act. His Yea on it is set aside. Read as rung 2, it would make his record two-sided (doesnt-fit). His own words concern HB 1235 (2024, only the state may sue gun makers), which is about which level may sue: adjacent.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor and Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (68-30, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" is a capitol carry right for state officials, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban ("shall not knowingly or willfully keep" … any list, record, or registry of privately owned firearms) repeals no permit or registration requirement (66-26). Furthest act: 5. Under the orchestrator note (runner ruling pending), HB 1365 (2023, machine-gun definition widened to conversion parts) is treated as no rung act. His Yea on it is set aside. Read as rung 2, it would make his record two-sided (doesnt-fit). His own words concern HB 1235 (2024, only the state may sue gun makers), which is about which level may sue: adjacent.$r4$),
  ('4f57bead-e464-4a26-bc14-49f9599f2086', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of IN HB 1296 (2022) and Yea on its House conference report, 2022-03-08. HB 1296 (2022) repeals the licence offence ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and lists who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; it removes the permit to carry for adults, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Its list of persons barred from permit-free carry is the condition that the permit-removal law sets on its own permit-free carry, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3), not a separate rung 3 act. Single-subject verdict (season 3, codebook 0.6), census gate none. His own words back it: words to the same effect (no readable page prints them) (caucus release, 2022-01-11). He also voted Yea on rung 4 acts (HB 1071 2017, HB 1084 2024, SB 43 2017); furthest act is 5. His Yea on HB 1235 (2024, lawsuit preemption) is adjacent; no restriction-side rung act is ungated. A Yea on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note); if it were read as rung 2, this record would become two-sided and the row a BLANK adjacent-chairs.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of IN HB 1296 (2022) and Yea on its House conference report, 2022-03-08. HB 1296 (2022) repeals the licence offence ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and lists who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; it removes the permit to carry for adults, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Its list of persons barred from permit-free carry is the condition that the permit-removal law sets on its own permit-free carry, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3), not a separate rung 3 act. Single-subject verdict (season 3, codebook 0.6), census gate none. His own words back it: words to the same effect (no readable page prints them) (caucus release, 2022-01-11). He also voted Yea on HB 1071 2017, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on, and on HB 1084 2024 and SB 43 2017, capitol carry rights for state officials, which are out of scope (not rung acts); furthest act is 5. His Yea on HB 1235 (2024, lawsuit preemption) is adjacent; no restriction-side rung act is ungated. A Yea on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note); if it were read as rung 2, this record would become two-sided and the row a BLANK adjacent-chairs.$r4$),
  ('4fb912dc-b35c-42da-888a-f3e58cdbf97c', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (a 2025 shooting-range bill) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). He co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. He voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only). His own words ("from universal background checks to red flag laws", 2023; "Enacted universal background checks and red flag laws", 2024) back the same rung 3 acts.$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling this shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). These conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (a 2025 shooting-range bill) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). He co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. He voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only). His own words ("from universal background checks to red flag laws", 2023; "Enacted universal background checks and red flag laws", 2024) back the same rung 3 acts.$r4$),
  ('5643823e-eaf1-4501-85a8-045c1dcbb013', 4,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (63-46), and co-sponsor of HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state (floor vote 60-45, so not gated). These keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His other records are No votes on rung 3 restrictions (direction only), a gated Yea on HB 4481, and a No on SB 22 (no rung act); his own words against a type ban and red-flag orders show the same side but no rung, and the armed-staff program line is third-person release text with no bill number.$r4$,
