  ('5baf0e48-0e1a-4af5-bc7f-49257063c2a3', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4003, HB 4030, HB 4284, HB 4980, HB 5113, HB 5187 and SB 11 (direction only). Her 2026 shooting-penalty bills have no file in the store and are sentencing (adjacent); a 2024 House resolution has no file; her 2026 and campaign-site words name no rung policy.$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling this shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). These conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4003, HB 4030, HB 4284, HB 4980, HB 5113, HB 5187 and SB 11 (direction only). Her 2026 shooting-penalty bills have no file in the store and are sentencing (adjacent); a 2024 House resolution has no file; her 2026 and campaign-site words name no rung policy.$r4$),
  ('5c6cb815-dfae-4883-91cf-c6428c7e2444', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the final Senate vote (30-20) for HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. He also authored (Senate) and voted Yea on HB 1084 (2024): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (carry allowed in a public building where it was barred, rung 4; the registry and payment-code sections are not rung acts), and voted Yea on HB 1071 and SB 43 (2017), rung 4 acts on the same side. His HB 1284 (2019) authorship is a multi-subject bill. No ungated restriction-side rung act.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the final Senate vote (30-20) for HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. He also authored (Senate) and voted Yea on HB 1084 (2024): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope and not a rung act; the registry and payment-code sections are not rung acts), and voted Yea on HB 1071 (2017), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act, and on SB 43 (2017), a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act). His HB 1284 (2019) authorship is a multi-subject bill. No ungated restriction-side rung act.$r4$),
  ('5d57215a-7a47-4650-9c7b-47ff41d6322b', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (30-20, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried (the carry-easier rung); its registry ban ("shall not knowingly or willfully keep" … any list, record, or registry of privately owned firearms) repeals no permit or registration requirement (39-9) is on the same side; under furthest act the record sits at the permit-repeal rung. His restriction-side votes (HB 1651, SB 119, HB 1365) are all gated near-unanimous; his Nay on HB 1249 (2026) is on a multi-subject bill.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (30-20, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" is a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban ("shall not knowingly or willfully keep" … any list, record, or registry of privately owned firearms) repeals no permit or registration requirement (39-9) is on the same side; under furthest act the record sits at the permit-repeal rung. His restriction-side votes (HB 1651, SB 119, HB 1365) are all gated near-unanimous; his Nay on HB 1249 (2026) is on a multi-subject bill.$r4$),
  ('60a1cd0b-9c8b-4c64-beec-4c759732937a', 4,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00", HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee" and HB 4030 (2021), the licence fee cut from "$100.00" to "not more than $45.00" (an easier carry permit, rung 4 under annex rule two); Yea on HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"; Yea on HB 5187 (2021), an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm" and SB 11 (2021), an emergency order does not affect a county clerk's duty to ... Issue a concealed pistol license (no carry ban or permit stop during a declared emergency, rung 4 under annex the licence rule). The permit stays in every bill; split House votes, single-subject. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), which shows a side only, and no record repeals a permit: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier. HB 4003 (2021) and HB 4434 (2019) only change a gun-crime penalty (adjacent). SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00", and HB 4030 (2021), the licence fee cut from "$100.00" to "not more than $45.00" (an easier carry permit, rung 4 under annex rule two); Yea on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee"; Yea on HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"; Yea on HB 5187 (2021), an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm" and SB 11 (2021), an emergency order does not affect a county clerk's duty to ... Issue a concealed pistol license (no carry ban or permit stop during a declared emergency). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 4980, HB 5113, HB 4855, HB 5187 and SB 11 show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. The permit stays in every bill; split House votes, single-subject. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), which shows a side only, and no record repeals a permit: rung 4 on the two fee cuts, make carrying a gun easier. HB 4003 (2021) and HB 4434 (2019) only change a gun-crime penalty (adjacent). SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$),
  ('60dec8ad-4ae4-4ff8-bf99-a4d5df5aeb7b', 5,
