   $r4$Co-sponsor of, and Yea on the final House vote (conference report, 68-30) for, HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit for a gun, such as the permit to carry. His Yea and co-sponsorship of HB 1084 (2024): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope and not a rung act; the registry and payment-code sections are not rung acts) does not count toward the seat. No restriction-side rung act is ungated (his HB 1365 vote was No).$r4$),
  ('687ef2ea-aecc-4140-8374-28d855a4cef1', 3,
   $r4$His own answer to the 2022 questionnaire item 'What restrictions on gun ownership are needed to protect public safety?' names 'age restrictions for handguns and background checks that also include the minor records of a person under the age of 22 applying to purchase firearms': a minimum age and a check on a set of buyers, rung 3 'Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun'. Date is the 2022 race (the campaign that first seated him; flag for the code's election-cycle check). His in-term rung 4 Yeas and co-sponsorships keep the permit (HB 4284 fee cut, HB 4855 bow-hunter carry, HB 4980 apply in any county, HB 5113 free re-application), so under the licence rule (annex the licence rule) they do not conflict with a check or an age limit and the record sits at rung 3. His Nays on SB 471 (2023) and HB 4128 (2024) are direction-only and cancel nothing; his 2025 words ('I'd be a no vote' on an assault-weapons ban; laws 'never going to probably fix the problem') reject rung 2 and name no other policy.$r4$,
   $r4$His own answer to the 2022 questionnaire item 'What restrictions on gun ownership are needed to protect public safety?' names 'age restrictions for handguns and background checks that also include the minor records of a person under the age of 22 applying to purchase firearms': a minimum age and a check on a set of buyers, rung 3 'Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun'. Date is the 2022 race (the campaign that first seated him; flag for the code's election-cycle check). His in-term Yea on the rung 4 fee cut HB 4284 keeps the permit, so under the licence rule (annex the licence rule) it does not conflict with a check or an age limit. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling his Yeas and co-sponsorships on HB 4855 (bow-hunter carry), HB 4980 (apply in any county) and HB 5113 (free re-application) show only which side he is on, are not rung acts and conflict with nothing. The record sits at rung 3. His Nays on SB 471 (2023) and HB 4128 (2024) are direction-only and cancel nothing; his 2025 words ('I'd be a no vote' on an assault-weapons ban; laws 'never going to probably fix the problem') reject rung 2 and name no other policy.$r4$),
  ('68d580cc-e219-43d5-acce-c250140a3f0a', 4,
   $r4$As State Representative he co-sponsored MI HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote on file, so not gated), under which the Capitol Commission "shall not" … "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the State Capitol; he co-sponsored and voted Yea on HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45; apply to a county clerk in any county in this state) and HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13; carrying a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow"), and voted Yea on HB 4284 (CPL renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00") and HB 5113. These widen where licensees may carry or make the permit easier while it stays in place: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4). No act repeals a permit. His Yea on SB 22 (airport sterile area) copies a federal ban and is no rung act; his Nays on HB 4138, HB 4127, HB 4128, HB 4146 and SB 83 show a side only. HB 4481 is gated. His campaign-site line is third person and not used.$r4$,
