   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling these show only which side she is on and are not rung acts: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27). These conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (direction only); her No on HB 4481 is gated (92-9). A 2024 House resolution she sponsored has no file in the store; her WOOD TV words are undated and name no policy.$r4$),
  ('77261f08-6172-4a58-a5d6-446c2b84a50e', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor and Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (68-30, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yeas on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried (the carry-easier rung); its registry ban ("shall not knowingly or willfully keep" … any list, record, or registry of privately owned firearms) repeals no permit or registration requirement, HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a civil protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days", while the licence system stays: the carry-easier rung and SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building": the carry-easier rung are on the same side (furthest act: 5). His 2021 words ("The right to own a handgun without needing a state-issued license is already allowed in many parts of the country") agree. Under the orchestrator note (runner ruling pending), HB 1365 (2023, machine-gun definition widened to conversion parts) is treated as no rung act. His Yea on it is set aside. Read as rung 2, it would make his record two-sided (doesnt-fit). His restriction-side votes are gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor and Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (68-30, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yeas on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" is a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban ("shall not knowingly or willfully keep" … any list, record, or registry of privately owned firearms) repeals no permit or registration requirement, HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a civil protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days", while the licence system stays: since the 2026-11-12 ruling this shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; and SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building": a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act). The seat rests on HB 1296 (furthest act: 5). His 2021 words ("The right to own a handgun without needing a state-issued license is already allowed in many parts of the country") agree. Under the orchestrator note (runner ruling pending), HB 1365 (2023, machine-gun definition widened to conversion parts) is treated as no rung act. His Yea on it is set aside. Read as rung 2, it would make his record two-sided (doesnt-fit). His restriction-side votes are gated.$r4$),
  ('786e6ab8-bfe6-453b-a361-503b6e8b0cb0', 4,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00", HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee": each makes the carry permit easier to get (a lower fee, a nearer office, no new fee), rung 4 under annex rule two. Yea on HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow", which widens who may carry (rung 4). Single-subject bills on split House votes; the permit stays. She voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), which shows a side only, and no record repeals a permit, so the furthest act is rung 4: make carrying a gun easier. SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00": it makes the carry permit easier to get (a lower fee), rung 4 under annex rule two. Yea also on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee": these change only where and how the paperwork is handled, so since the 2026-11-12 ruling they show only which side she is on and are not rung acts. Yea on HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow", a carry change for one narrow situation, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act. Single-subject bills on split House votes; the permit stays. She voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), which shows a side only, and no record repeals a permit, so the furthest act is rung 4: make carrying a gun easier. SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$),
  ('79bb9e5a-a74a-44ef-a1c4-5b956ad36e5e', 5,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". His Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by a 2019 bill and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". His Yea on HB 1071 (2017) shows only which side he is on and, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, is not a rung act. His Yeas on SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) back capitol carry rights for officials, which are out of scope (not rung acts). The seat rests on HB 1296, so the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by a 2019 bill and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$),
  ('7ac75846-3990-45d0-b74a-b85d6d672f61', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Same-side acts: author and Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried while the permit system stays (rung 4); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; author and Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays (rung 4); co-sponsor and Yea on SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (rung 4). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His authorships of HB 1095 (2017, armor-piercing ammunition: not a firearm or magazine type), HB 1137 (2025, red-flag record expungement) and SB 13 (2017) state no rung act. No own words on the question were found.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Other bills: author and Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" is a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; author and Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays; since the 2026-11-12 ruling this shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; co-sponsor and Yea on SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His authorships of HB 1095 (2017, armor-piercing ammunition: not a firearm or magazine type), HB 1137 (2025, red-flag record expungement) and SB 13 (2017) state no rung act. No own words on the question were found.$r4$),
