e set aside. His authorship of HB 1296 is therefore not used; the seat rests on the conference-report vote. His 2021-2022 own words ("law-abiding folks should be able to carry a handgun and they should be able to do so without asking for a permit from the government") match rung 5 but date from an earlier term, so they are not relied on. His No on HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act; HB 1137 (2025) authorship states no rung act.$r4$),
  ('81db8d2b-97bb-49f4-8bf1-ab3bf11b5f08', 4,
   $r4$Every counted loosening act keeps the permit and makes carrying easier: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (carry allowed where it was barred, permit system kept: rung 4); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (applying at a nearer office, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (an easier permit, a fee waiver, annex rule two: rung 4); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). His No votes on the 2023 restrictions and on SB 22 show a direction only; HB 4481 is gated. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$,
   $r4$Every loosening act keeps the permit: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (a carry change for one narrow situation; since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (a change to where the paperwork is handled; since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (a free re-application after an error; since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). His No votes on the 2023 restrictions and on SB 22 show a direction only; HB 4481 is gated. The rung 4 acts are HB 4284 and HB 4943. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$),
  ('86106451-e701-496a-b182-468b76a8d3e1', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). He co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. He voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only).$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Yea also on HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13), a carry change for one narrow situation: since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side he is on, is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing. No rung 4 Yea is in the record, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). He co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. He voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only).$r4$),
  ('881e36c7-fb6a-4f67-b64c-1ed6a1182764', 4,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of and Yea on HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; prime sponsor of and Yea on HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; co-sponsor of and Yea on HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00"; Yea on HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00", on HB 5113 (2025) lets an applicant whose licence was refused for an application error "submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", and on SB 11 (2021), which keeps a clerk's duty to "Issue a concealed pistol license" in an emergency. These keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His votes on rung 3 restrictions are all No (direction only), his No on SB 22 is no rung act, and no statement of his own was found.$r4$,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of and Yea on HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; prime sponsor of and Yea on HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; co-sponsor of and Yea on HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00"; Yea on HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00", on HB 5113 (2025) lets an applicant whose licence was refused for an application error "submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", and on SB 11 (2021), which keeps a clerk's duty to "Issue a concealed pistol license" in an emergency. HB 4284 and HB 4030 keep the concealed pistol licence and make the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (where and how the paperwork is handled) and HB 5187 and SB 11 (emergency powers) show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. His votes on rung 3 restrictions are all No (direction only), his No on SB 22 is no rung act, and no statement of his own was found.$r4$),
  ('89648ec2-7768-41b2-8dfd-4e8297f3d3b2', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of, and Yea on the final House vote (68-30) for, HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. His Yeas on HB 1084 (2024), HB 1071 and SB 43 (2017) are rung 4 acts on the same side; his No on HB 1365 (2023) is consistent. His 2026 questionnaire answers ("Current laws are sufficient."; teachers carrying: "Yes With proper training.") lean the same way and do not contradict the seat.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of, and Yea on the final House vote (68-30) for, HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject,. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. His Yea on HB 1071 (2017) shows only which side he is on and, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, is not a rung act; his Yeas on SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) back capitol carry rights for officials, which are out of scope (not rung acts); his No on HB 1365 (2023) is consistent. His 2026 questionnaire answers ("Current laws are sufficient."; teachers carrying: "Yes With proper training.") lean the same way and do not contradict the seat.$r4$),
  ('8a6e0f99-acde-4ce1-a15a-cef54b7d8ad9', 4,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; co-sponsor of HB 4943 (the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol); co-sponsor and Yea on HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state); Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}") and HB 4855. These widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. No rung 5 act; his Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only, and his 2024 voter-guide 'Yes' to teachers carrying agrees. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 5187 (2022, 61-40): an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm"; co-sponsor of HB 4943 (the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol); co-sponsor and Yea on HB 4980 ("an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state); Yeas on HB 4284 (renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}") and HB 4855. HB 4943 and HB 4284 widen carry or ease the permit while it stays: 'Make carrying a gun easier'. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 5187 (emergency powers), HB 4980 (where the paperwork is handled) and HB 4855 (a pistol while bow hunting) show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. No rung 5 act; his Nays on the 2023 restriction bills show a side only, and his 2024 voter-guide 'Yes' to teachers carrying agrees. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$),
  ('8af9732c-8050-4732-acdf-6e2c8ed6e30b', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Same-side act: Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried while the permit system stays (rung 4); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement (39-9). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and a 2017 bill copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, a 2023 bill is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His SB 119 co-sponsorship and his SB 158 (2023), HB 1284 (2019) and HB 1343 (2026) authorships are set aside under those rulings; HB 1177 (2023) is multi-subject. He abstained on HB 1235 (2024), which is adjacent (lawsuits against gun makers). No own words were found.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Other bill: Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" is a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement (39-9). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and a 2017 bill copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, a 2023 bill is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His SB 119 co-sponsorship and his SB 158 (2023), HB 1284 (2019) and HB 1343 (2026) authorships are set aside under those rulings; HB 1177 (2023) is multi-subject. He abstained on HB 1235 (2024), which is adjacent (lawsuits against gun makers). No own words were found.$r4$),
  ('8b8f9f4a-fa69-4725-ba4d-f20f4090abe5', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 place ban HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 acts HB 4284 (2026, 63-46: the licence renewal fee falls from $115.00 to $30.00); HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. His Nay on HB 4980 shows a side only; HB 4481 is gated (92-9); HB 4949, HB 5450 (storage notice) and HB 6145/6146 (disposal of forfeited guns) are adjacent.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 place ban HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 4284 (2026, 63-46: the licence renewal fee falls from $115.00 to $30.00), which keeps the permit. Yea too on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow") and HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"): since the 2026-11-12 ruling these show only which side he is on, are not rung acts and conflict with nothing. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 act sits beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. His Nay on HB 4980 shows a side only; HB 4481 is gated (92-9); HB 4949, HB 5450 (storage notice) and HB 6145/6146 (disposal of forfeited guns) are adjacent.$r4$),
  ('8b98d8e9-c41c-4722-80de-3bc5192656ee', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 30-20, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His other ungated Yeas are rung 4 acts on the same side: HB 1071 (2017) lets protection-order holders carry without a licence for 60 days ("A person may carry a handgun without a license if the person" … "is protected by a protection order"), a carry widening for one group while the licence system stays: rung 4 (Senate, 38-12); HB 1084 (2024) says "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", which widens where a gun may be carried: rung 4 (its registry and payment-code parts state no rung act) (Senate, 39-9); SB 43 (2017) says a legislator "who: (1) possesses a valid Indiana license to carry a handgun" … "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", which widens where a gun may be carried while the licence system stays: rung 4 (Senate concurrence, 39-7). Furthest act → 5. His HB-1177-2023 authorship is a multi-subject bill (direction only); his HB-1651-2019 Yea is gated (46-2); his own words on teacher training state no rung.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 30-20, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His other ungated Yeas are not rung acts: HB 1071 (2017) lets protection-order holders carry without a licence for 60 days ("A person may carry a handgun without a license if the person" … "is protected by a protection order"), a carry change for one narrow situation while the licence system stays, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on (Senate, 38-12); HB 1084 (2024) says "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (its registry and payment-code parts state no rung act) (Senate, 39-9); SB 43 (2017) says a legislator "who: (1) possesses a valid Indiana license to carry a handgun" … "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (Senate concurrence, 39-7). Furthest act → 5. His HB-1177-2023 authorship is a multi-subject bill (direction only); his HB-1651-2019 Yea is gated (46-2); his own words on teacher training state no rung.$r4$),
  ('8bd4e2e7-cf6d-4029-9eaa-b05aa2f753e5', 4,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of MI HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote on file, not gated): the Capitol Commission "shall not enact any rule" … "to prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol; Yea on HB 4284 (2026, 63-46), renewal fee cut "[-$115.00 -]{+$30.00 +}"; Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (60-45), "an individual shall apply to" … a county clerk in any county in this state; Yea on HB 4855 (90-13), does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "(c) The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"; Yea on HB 5113 (fee waiver on reapplying). Each widens carry or eases the permit while it stays, meeting 'Make carrying a gun easier' (annex rule two). No rung 5 act. His Nays on the 2023 check, red-flag and polling-place bills show a side only; his 'due process' words on red-flag orders agree. MI-SB-22-2023 (airport sterile areas) is read as no rung act: its store title says it "clarify"s an existing prohibition and provides penalties, and the text does not show the ban as new. HB 4481 (2025, 92-9) is near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
