  ('8d0af843-f21e-4ce8-8f6f-415fc4838f22', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Same-side acts: author and Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried while the permit system stays (rung 4); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays (rung 4); co-sponsor of SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (rung 4). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His SB 119 (2019) authorship is set aside under that ruling. His 2017 floor words on staff carry in the Statehouse lean the