only carry bills are set aside. His SB 119 (2019) authorship is set aside under that ruling. His 2017 floor words on staff carry in the Statehouse lean the same way and name no further rung.$r4$),
  ('8dbec2f8-1ee0-44f6-8384-b96c4252d2b0', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. Her Nay on HB 4284 shows a side only; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; her quoted words (background checks "are not a panacea") name no rung.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"), each keeping the permit. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling this act shows only which side she is on, is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. Her Nay on HB 4284 shows a side only; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; her quoted words (background checks "are not a panacea") name no rung.$r4$),
  ('8e050c00-d5d8-447a-8a50-3af1688c7aea', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor and Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (68-30, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a civil protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days", while the licence system stays: the carry-easier rung and SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building": the carry-easier rung are on the same side (furthest act: 5). Under the orchestrator note (runner ruling pending), HB 1365 (2023, machine-gun definition widened to conversion parts) is treated as no rung act. His Yea on it is set aside. Read as rung 2, it would make his record two-sided (doesnt-fit). His restriction-side votes are gated. No own words were found.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor and Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject: it ends the licence to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; the annex places a state law that removes its own carry permit on the permit-repeal rung, and the prohibited-carrier list is part of that act (closure C3 reading) (68-30, 2022-03-08, ungated): it meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a civil protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days", while the licence system stays: since the 2026-11-12 ruling this shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; and SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building": a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act). The seat rests on HB 1296 (furthest act: 5). Under the orchestrator note (runner ruling pending), HB 1365 (2023, machine-gun definition widened to conversion parts) is treated as no rung act. His Yea on it is set aside. Read as rung 2, it would make his record two-sided (doesnt-fit). His restriction-side votes are gated. No own words were found.$r4$),
  ('8f1088b4-48b5-40ed-95e6-16d8dabd2d33', 5,
   $r4$Senate Yea (30-20) on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". His Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act is 5. His sponsorships known only by LegiScan caption, and his gated votes, are not relied on. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$,
   $r4$Senate Yea (30-20) on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". His Yea on HB 1071 (2017) shows only which side he is on and, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, is not a rung act. His Yeas on SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) back capitol carry rights for officials, which are out of scope (not rung acts). The seat rests on HB 1296, so the furthest act is 5. His sponsorships known only by LegiScan caption, and his gated votes, are not relied on. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$),
  ('946f7bc2-48ed-447e-a9a5-cb99aae00b31', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the final House vote (conference report, 68-30) for HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject, and co-sponsor of it. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. His Yeas on HB 1084 (2024): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (carry allowed in a public building where it was barred, rung 4; the registry and payment-code sections are not rung acts) and on SB 43 (2017) are rung 4 acts on the same side. He co-sponsored and voted Yea on the final House vote (concurrence, 71-23) for HB 1365 (2023, machine guns), which by orchestrator note (runner ruling pending) is no rung act: it neither seats nor makes a record two-sided. No other ungated restriction-side rung act. He is a House member (District 40), as the store records show.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the final House vote (conference report, 68-30) for HB 1296 (2022), whose operative text repeals the licence to carry a handgun (the offence of "carrying a handgun without a license" is listed "(before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3), single-subject, and co-sponsor of it. That meets rung 5, repeal laws that require a permit to carry. His Yeas on HB 1084 (2024): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (a capitol carry right for state officials; the registry and payment-code sections are not rung acts) and on SB 43 (2017), a capitol carry right for lawmakers, are out of scope (not rung acts). He co-sponsored and voted Yea on the final House vote (concurrence, 71-23) for HB 1365 (2023, machine guns), which by orchestrator note (runner ruling pending) is no rung act: it neither seats nor makes a record two-sided. No other ungated restriction-side rung act. He is a House member (District 40), as the store records show.$r4$),
  ('95cf3f0c-03a0-4031-be8b-24e7aaa611a2', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; a 2025 reciprocity-website bill is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only). Her own words ("I am in favor of universal background checks", "red flag laws", 2023-2024) back the same rung 3 acts.$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Yea also on HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13), a carry change for one narrow situation: since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side she is on, is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing. No rung 4 Yea is in the record, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; a 2025 reciprocity-website bill is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only). Her own words ("I am in favor of universal background checks", "red flag laws", 2023-2024) back the same rung 3 acts.$r4$),
  ('97c61094-b962-48b2-b6ef-de96b5f9bb7a', 5,
   $r4$Yea in the Senate on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Senate Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) (rung 4) sit below it under furthest act. His 2018 op-ed and 2022 AP quote are consistent but out of the current cycle and not relied on.$r4$,
