tor note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. If HB 1365 (2023) were read as a rung 2 act (its machine-gun definition), this person's ungated Yea on it would sit beside the rung 5 act on the other side of the middle, and the code would change to doesnt-fit (two-sided).$r4$),
  ('9f12fc5f-4827-443d-95da-80c66ca11ebf', 3,
   $r4$Senate record: Yea on SB 76 (2023), no one may "Purchase or acquire a firearm that is not a pistol without a license as prescribed in this section", and on HB 4138 (checking every buyer); co-sponsor and Yea on SB 83 (2023), "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding, and Yea on HB 4146 (red-flag orders); Yea on SB 331 (2025), a person shall not "manufacture or assemble" … "sell" … "any firearm that is not imprinted with a valid serial number" (serial numbers on every gun); and Yea on HB 4127, HB 4128 and SB 225/226 (2025), a person shall not "possess a firearm on the premises of" … "The Michigan State Capitol Building" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject and divided (19-17 to 22-16). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: as a House member, Yea on HB 5187 (2021), an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm (2022, 61-40), lifting a carry ban during a declared emergency. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450) and the expired-CPL penalty (HB 4003, HB 4434, which he prime-sponsored) are adjacent. Earlier-chamber House records count (V5). SB 1086 (voluntary do-not-sell list) is direction-only; the bump-stock bans SB 942 and SB 224 are not rung 2 (an accessory, annex rule three); SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans (no rung act). His Nos on HB 4030 and a 2021 emergency-carry bill show a side only. His 2025 newsletter backs the same 2023 package.$r4$,
   $r4$Senate record: Yea on SB 76 (2023), no one may "Purchase or acquire a firearm that is not a pistol without a license as prescribed in this section", and on HB 4138 (checking every buyer); co-sponsor and Yea on SB 83 (2023), "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding, and Yea on HB 4146 (red-flag orders); Yea on SB 331 (2025), a person shall not "manufacture or assemble" … "sell" … "any firearm that is not imprinted with a valid serial number" (serial numbers on every gun); and Yea on HB 4127, HB 4128 and SB 225/226 (2025), a person shall not "possess a firearm on the premises of" … "The Michigan State Capitol Building" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject and divided (19-17 to 22-16). As a House member he voted Yea on HB 5187 (2021), an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm (2022, 61-40), an emergency-powers bill: since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side he is on, is not a rung act and conflicts with nothing. No rung 4 Yea is in the record, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450) and the expired-CPL penalty (HB 4003, HB 4434, which he prime-sponsored) are adjacent. Earlier-chamber House records count (V5). SB 1086 (voluntary do-not-sell list) is direction-only; the bump-stock bans SB 942 and SB 224 are not rung 2 (an accessory, annex rule three); SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans (no rung act). His Nos on HB 4030 and a 2021 emergency-carry bill show a side only. His 2025 newsletter backs the same 2023 package.$r4$),
  ('a7a31331-5804-4826-9565-a44422d5858e', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146. His 2023 words, "We must pass legislation that implements universal background checks on all gun purchases, safe storage laws and red flag laws", agree with the record. His Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated.$r4$,
