  ('afb41984-f662-4560-84f0-75e59a55960f', 4,
   $r4$Every counted loosening act keeps the permit and makes carrying easier: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (carry allowed where it was barred, permit system kept: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (applying at a nearer office, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (an easier permit, a fee waiver, annex rule two: rung 4); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). Her votes against the 2023 checks, red-flag and place-ban bills show a direction only. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner. HB 4481 (vote and sponsorship) is gated near-unanimous. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$,
   $r4$Every loosening act keeps the permit: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (a carry change for one narrow situation; since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (a change to where the paperwork is handled; since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (a free re-application after an error; since the 2026-11-12 ruling it shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). Her votes against the 2023 checks, red-flag and place-ban bills show a direction only. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner. HB 4481 (vote and sponsorship) is gated near-unanimous. The rung 4 acts are HB 4284 and HB 4943. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$),
  ('b5a9a489-0784-428a-b1ed-81fd40e07ff5', 5,
   $r4$Yea on IN HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, conference report 68-30 House / 30-20 Senate: it repeals the licence to carry (the text cites "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and repeals IC 35-47-2-2), replacing it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; that list is the permit-free carry rule's own eligibility line, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3): rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". Yea on IN HB 1084 (2024), single-subject, 66-26 House conference report (39-9 Senate): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building and on the property of the state capitol complex." is a rung 4 act on the same side; furthest act gives 5. Her LegiScan co-sponsorship of HB 1296 is not used: the bill passed the House 91-0 before the permit repeal was added in conference, so the version she co-sponsored is not the stored text. Her 2026 voter-guide words "I support the 2nd amendment" show a side only; the 2016 "Disagree" is out of cycle. Other gun findings are gated (near-unanimous or multi-subject) or out of scope (adjacent: shooting-range zoning SB 176, insurer gun-free-zone signs HB 1274, licence-holder privacy HB 1323, local suits against the industry HB 1235, police gun trades HB 1143); a vote on a related bill (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on IN HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, conference report 68-30 House / 30-20 Senate: it repeals the licence to carry (the text cites "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and repeals IC 35-47-2-2), replacing it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; that list is the permit-free carry rule's own eligibility line, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3): rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". Yea on IN HB 1084 (2024), single-subject, 66-26 House conference report (39-9 Senate): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building and on the property of the state capitol complex." is a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); furthest act gives 5. Her LegiScan co-sponsorship of HB 1296 is not used: the bill passed the House 91-0 before the permit repeal was added in conference, so the version she co-sponsored is not the stored text. Her 2026 voter-guide words "I support the 2nd amendment" show a side only; the 2016 "Disagree" is out of cycle. Other gun findings are gated (near-unanimous or multi-subject) or out of scope (adjacent: shooting-range zoning SB 176, insurer gun-free-zone signs HB 1274, licence-holder privacy HB 1323, local suits against the industry HB 1235, police gun trades HB 1143); a vote on a related bill (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided.$r4$),
  ('b79f38f2-02a9-4659-b4df-9e12535a2255', 5,
   $r4$Re-coded under the Session C sponsorship gate. Yea on the House conference report of IN HB 1296 (2022-03-08, 68-30, single-subject, not gated), which repeals the licence requirement to carry a handgun ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun": repealing the permit to carry, rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". The list of barred persons is part of the permit-free carry rule (closure C3). Her Yeas on rung 4 acts HB 1071 (2017), HB 1084 (2024) and SB 43 (2017) sit on the same side. Her record is no longer two-sided: her co-sponsorship of HB 1651 (2019, red-flag possession ban, rung 3) is gated because both counted final votes are near-unanimous (93-1, 46-2), and a gated sponsorship does not make a record two-sided. No other ungated rung 1-3 act stands: HB 1558 (task force) and HB 1137 are gated; her SB 158, SB 335 and HB 1240 sponsorships are multi-subject (direction only); HB 1365 and SB 119 are no rung act (Indiana rulings).$r4$,
