  ('bba53cbb-15de-432f-9d6e-d0ab431eac15', 5,
   $r4$Yea in the House on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Yea on HB 1084 (2024) (rung 4) sits below it.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea in the House on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Yea on HB 1084 (2024) is a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act).$r4$),
  ('be36fee6-f81b-4e61-953c-0e89f9718bb4', 5,
   $r4$In the House (2017; same legislature, in-term) he voted Yea (59-49) on HB 4416, a single-subject bill that makes the concealed-weapon ban "not apply to" … "(a) A firearm" and keeps a concealed-pistol ban only for "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm", deleting the words "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law": a repeal of the permit to carry (rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"; furthest act over his rung 4 Yeas on HB 4030, HB 5187 and SB 11). No rung 1-3 act stands beside it: his Senate records are No votes, SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act, and his two 2017 Yeas on near-unanimous gun bills are gated. The two Senate package records name only the first bill of each package, so they are unverified and are not relied on; his own words on those packages support the same side.$r4$,
   $r4$In the House (2017; same legislature, in-term) he voted Yea (59-49) on HB 4416, a single-subject bill that makes the concealed-weapon ban "not apply to" … "(a) A firearm" and keeps a concealed-pistol ban only for "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm", deleting the words "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law": a repeal of the permit to carry (rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"; furthest act over his rung 4 Yea on HB 4030; his Yeas on HB 5187 and SB 11 show only which side he is on since the 2026-11-12 ruling). No rung 1-3 act stands beside it: his Senate records are No votes, SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act, and his two 2017 Yeas on near-unanimous gun bills are gated. The two Senate package records name only the first bill of each package, so they are unverified and are not relied on; his own words on those packages support the same side.$r4$),
  ('befd0035-e611-4d14-9da9-227156bb2c10', 5,
   $r4$Yea on IN HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, conference report 68-30 House / 30-20 Senate: it repeals the licence to carry (the text cites "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and repeals IC 35-47-2-2), replacing it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; that list is the permit-free carry rule's own eligibility line, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3): rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". Yeas on IN HB 1084 (2024), single-subject, 66-26 House conference report (39-9 Senate): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building and on the property of the state capitol complex." and IN SB 43 (2017), single-subject, 76-16 House / Senate concurrence 39-7: "A member of the general assembly who: (1) possesses a valid Indiana license to carry a handgun" … "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" are rung 4 acts on the same side. Her Nay on the HB 1071 (2017) conference report is a No on a different bill (direction-only) and cancels no Yea. Her Yea on SB 344 (2017) is near-unanimous (91-1), gated. Other gun findings are gated (near-unanimous or multi-subject) or out of scope (adjacent: shooting-range zoning SB 176, insurer gun-free-zone signs HB 1274, licence-holder privacy HB 1323, local suits against the industry HB 1235, police gun trades HB 1143); a vote on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. If HB 1365 (2023) were read as a rung 2 act (its machine-gun definition), this person's ungated Yea on it would sit beside the rung 5 act on the other side of the middle, and the code would change to doesnt-fit (two-sided).$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on IN HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, conference report 68-30 House / 30-20 Senate: it repeals the licence to carry (the text cites "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and repeals IC 35-47-2-2), replacing it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; that list is the permit-free carry rule's own eligibility line, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3): rung 5 "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". Yeas on IN HB 1084 (2024), single-subject, 66-26 House conference report (39-9 Senate): "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building and on the property of the state capitol complex." and IN SB 43 (2017), single-subject, 76-16 House / Senate concurrence 39-7: "A member of the general assembly who: (1) possesses a valid Indiana license to carry a handgun" … "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" give a capitol carry right for state officials and lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act). Her Nay on the HB 1071 (2017) conference report is a No on a different bill (direction-only) and cancels no Yea. Her Yea on SB 344 (2017) is near-unanimous (91-1), gated. Other gun findings are gated (near-unanimous or multi-subject) or out of scope (adjacent: shooting-range zoning SB 176, insurer gun-free-zone signs HB 1274, licence-holder privacy HB 1323, local suits against the industry HB 1235, police gun trades HB 1143); a vote on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note, runner ruling pending): it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. If HB 1365 (2023) were read as a rung 2 act (its machine-gun definition), this person's ungated Yea on it would sit beside the rung 5 act on the other side of the middle, and the code would change to doesnt-fit (two-sided).$r4$),
  ('c0ba1bd4-09ad-43ff-b9b9-f825d47eb330', 4,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00" and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow": an easier carry permit and a wider class of carriers, rung 4 (annex rule two), with the permit kept. Single-subject bills on split House votes. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), which shows a side only, and no record repeals a permit: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier. SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00" and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow". HB 4284, a lower fee, makes the carry permit easier to get with the permit kept: rung 4 (annex rule two). Since the 2026-11-12 ruling, HB 5113 and HB 4980 (how and where the paperwork is handled) and HB 4855 (a pistol while bow hunting) show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. Single-subject bills on split House votes. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), which shows a side only, and no record repeals a permit: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier. SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$),
  ('c0be6d9e-5e15-4420-bf5e-3cfee27e762d', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 acts HB 4980 (2026, 60-45: an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She co-sponsored HB 4146 and HB 4127. Her Nays on other carry bills (HB 4030, HB 4284, HB 4855, HB 5187, SB 11) show a side only. Her 2024 op-ed calling universal background checks and extreme risk protection orders "absolutely necessary" agrees with the record.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4980 (2026, 60-45: an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Since the 2026-11-12 ruling these acts show only which side she is on, are not rung acts and conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She co-sponsored HB 4146 and HB 4127. Her Nays on other carry bills (HB 4030, HB 4284, HB 4855, HB 5187, SB 11) show a side only. Her 2024 op-ed calling universal background checks and extreme risk protection orders "absolutely necessary" agrees with the record.$r4$),
  ('c0f3c9fd-0aeb-4dc2-a715-ef9e2d6cc4a7', 4,
   $r4$Yea on single-subject bills: HB 4030 (2021) cut the concealed pistol licence fee to "not more than $45.00"; HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00"; HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state (co-sponsor too); HB 4855 (2025) says the act does not prohibit "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow". He was prime sponsor and a Yea on HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order that would "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" … "of" … "A firearm", and co-sponsored HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote, so not gated) bars the Capitol Commission from rules that "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol. These keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His votes on rung 3 restrictions are all No (direction only) and his Yea on SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act; no statement of his own was found.$r4$,
