  ('cba4e1a1-d183-4a1e-93a4-80f075bd86bb', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 acts HB 4284 (2026, 63-46: the licence renewal fee falls from $115.00 to $30.00); HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 4980 (2026, 60-45: an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146, HB 4127 and HB 4128. His sponsorships of HB 5334 (2023) and a 2024 House resolution have no instrument file and cannot seat; HB 4481 is gated; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 4284 (2026, 63-46: the licence renewal fee falls from $115.00 to $30.00), which keeps the permit. Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 4980 (2026, 60-45: an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state); HB 5113 (2026, 78-27: an applicant may re-apply after an error "without paying an additional fee"), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts; they conflict with nothing. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 act sits beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146, HB 4127 and HB 4128. His sponsorships of HB 5334 (2023) and a 2024 House resolution have no instrument file and cannot seat; HB 4481 is gated; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans.$r4$),
  ('cd587673-42ec-4cf6-ac1e-acad01f58892', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only). Her 2023 newsletter words back background checks on purchases and ERPOs, the same rung 3 acts. A 2024 House resolution she sponsored has no file in the store.$r4$,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Yea also on HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act; it conflicts with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). She co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. She voted No on HB 4284, HB 4980 and HB 5113 (direction only). Her 2023 newsletter words back background checks on purchases and ERPOs, the same rung 3 acts. A 2024 House resolution she sponsored has no file in the store.$r4$),
  ('cd5fc12d-8d96-41e2-97ca-e00d28b1069a', 5,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". Her own words back the same act: "I am supporting legislation this session that would allow lawful Hoosiers to carry a handgun without having to get a government-issued license" (column, 2021-03-05) and, on HB 1077, "This legislation just removes unneeded paperwork and burdens for legal gun owners" (2022-01-12). Her Yea on HB 1084 (2024) is rung 4, same side, so the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by a 2019 bill and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Other ungated votes (a related bill, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". Her own words back the same act: "I am supporting legislation this session that would allow lawful Hoosiers to carry a handgun without having to get a government-issued license" (column, 2021-03-05) and, on HB 1077, "This legislation just removes unneeded paperwork and burdens for legal gun owners" (2022-01-12). Her Yea on HB 1084 (2024) gives a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by a 2019 bill and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Other ungated votes (a related bill, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$),
  ('cd8587a0-718b-4e9d-b4e1-f409884fa59f', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 acts HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 4980 (2026, 60-45: an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146, HB 4127 and HB 4128. He authored HB 4945 and HB 4946 (2023, domestic-violence misdemeanour bans), which have no instrument file and cannot seat (the enacted SB 471 copies a federal ban: no rung act). His Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"); HB 4980 (2026, 60-45: an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts; they conflict with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. He co-sponsored HB 4146, HB 4127 and HB 4128. He authored HB 4945 and HB 4946 (2023, domestic-violence misdemeanour bans), which have no instrument file and cannot seat (the enacted SB 471 copies a federal ban: no rung act). His Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated.$r4$),
  ('cde89f88-ac6f-429e-bc7d-e353de9bbeb5', 5,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 4416 (2017 House, 59-49, single-subject), which removes the licence to carry a concealed pistol: Sec. 227(3) leaves only "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm shall not carry a pistol concealed", deleting "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law". That meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit' … 'such as the permit to carry' (House record, same legislature as her Senate seat). Her other acts are on the same side (Yeas on HB 5187, SB 11, HB 4030 carry widenings; Nays on the 2023 check, red-flag and polling-place bills; a No shows a side only), and her 2023 words against red-flag orders and the Capitol carry ban agree. No rung 1-3 act; SB 219 and HB 4474 (2017) are near-unanimous and gated.$r4$,
   $r4$Prime sponsor of, and Yea on, MI HB 4416 (2017 House, 59-49, single-subject), which removes the licence to carry a concealed pistol: Sec. 227(3) leaves only "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm shall not carry a pistol concealed", deleting "without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law". That meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit' … 'such as the permit to carry' (House record, same legislature as her Senate seat). Her other acts are on the same side (Yea on HB 4030, a carry widening, and Yeas on HB 5187 and SB 11, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side she is on and are not rung acts; Nays on the 2023 check, red-flag and polling-place bills; a No shows a side only), and her 2023 words against red-flag orders and the Capitol carry ban agree. No rung 1-3 act; SB 219 and HB 4474 (2017) are near-unanimous and gated.$r4$),
  ('ce3c8481-f5d1-4be7-b50a-5d111b515190', 4,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00" and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow": an easier carry permit and a wider class of carriers, rung 4 (annex rule two), with the permit kept. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), and his 2025 words that red flag laws 'are a direct violation of our Second Amendment rights' back a repeal of red-flag orders, which annex Q4 reads as direction-only; no record repeals a permit. Furthest act: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier. SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00": an easier carry permit, rung 4 (annex rule two), with the permit kept. Yea on HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow": a free re-application, applying in any county and a pistol while bow hunting, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128), and his 2025 words that red flag laws 'are a direct violation of our Second Amendment rights' back a repeal of red-flag orders, which annex Q4 reads as direction-only; no record repeals a permit. Furthest act: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier. SB 22 (2023, weapons of every kind in an airport sterile area) is read as no rung act: it copies the federal sterile-area weapons ban, and its title says it clarifies an existing ban.$r4$),
  ('d2895028-7f86-4070-9665-e3175eef2cd0', 4,
   $r4$Yea on single-subject bills: HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00"; HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; HB 4855 (2025) says the act does not prohibit "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow" (he co-sponsored it); HB 5113 (2025) lets an applicant whose licence was refused for an application error "submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee". He co-sponsored HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote, so not gated) bars the Capitol Commission from rules that "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol. These keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His votes on rung 3 restrictions are all No (direction only), his HB 4481 acts are gated, and his Yea on SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act; no statement of his own was found.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on single-subject bills: HB 4284 (2025) cut the renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00"; HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; HB 4855 (2025) says the act does not prohibit "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow" (he co-sponsored it); HB 5113 (2025) lets an applicant whose licence was refused for an application error "submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee". He co-sponsored HB 4943 (2023, no floor vote, so not gated) bars the Capitol Commission from rules that "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol. HB 4980, HB 4855 and HB 5113, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. HB 4284 and HB 4943 keep the concealed pistol licence and make carrying easier or the permit easier to get (rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier"; annex rule two). His votes on rung 3 restrictions are all No (direction only), his HB 4481 acts are gated, and his Yea on SB 22 (2023), which restates the existing weapons ban in an airport sterile area and adds penalties, a ban federal law already has, is no rung act; no statement of his own was found.$r4$),
  ('d2b8e2ab-dc6b-42a9-9aea-b6b661e0476e', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 68-30, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His Yea on HB 1071 (2017) lets protection-order holders carry without a licence for 60 days ("A person may carry a handgun without a license if the person" … "is protected by a protection order"), a carry widening for one group while the licence system stays: rung 4 is a rung 4 act on the same side; furthest act → 5. He abstained (Speaker) on most later gun votes, which show nothing; his HB-1296 co-sponsorship is the insurance bill as filed, not a gun act; his Yeas on HB-1651-2019, SB-119-2019 and SB-344-2017 are gated or no rung acts. No own words on the subject were found.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022) (2022-03-08, 68-30, no gate). HB 1296 (2022) (conference report) ends the Indiana handgun licence requirement: its text refers to "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" and keeps only "a five (5) year or lifetime license to carry a handgun issued by the superintendent before July 1, 2022" valid; single-subject; the conference-report vote is the rung 5 act (runner ruling); its list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" is part of the permit-free carry rule (as closure C3), not a rung 3 act. That meets rung 5, repeal a law that requires a permit for a gun (the permit to carry). His Yea on HB 1071 (2017) lets protection-order holders carry without a licence for 60 days ("A person may carry a handgun without a license if the person" … "is protected by a protection order"), a carry change for one narrow situation while the licence system stays, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; furthest act → 5. He abstained (Speaker) on most later gun votes, which show nothing; his HB-1296 co-sponsorship is the insurance bill as filed, not a gun act; his Yeas on HB-1651-2019, SB-119-2019 and SB-344-2017 are gated or no rung acts. No own words on the subject were found.$r4$),
  ('d758604c-9dd9-4a00-ac09-90713a7bc4be', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (68-30, 2022-03-08, gate none). Same-side acts: Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried while the permit system stays (rung 4); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays (rung 4); House author and Yea on SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (rung 4). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His House authorships of SB 344 (2017) and SB 119 (2019) are set aside under that ruling, and so are his 2017 words for SB 344 ("If a person is here illegally or unlawfully, they do not have the right to possess or carry a firearm"): they back a ban federal law already has, so they state no rung 3 act (and are out of cycle). His HB 1077 (2022) co-sponsorship has no instrument file and cannot seat. His 2022 words for permit-free carry ("without having to wait weeks to receive a state-mandated license") match rung 5 but date from an earlier term, so they are not relied on.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the House conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (68-30, 2022-03-08, gate none). Other loosening findings: Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" gives a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays, a carry change for one narrow situation, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; House author and Yea on SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act). The only rung act left is the rung 5 act, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His House authorships of SB 344 (2017) and SB 119 (2019) are set aside under that ruling, and so are his 2017 words for SB 344 ("If a person is here illegally or unlawfully, they do not have the right to possess or carry a firearm"): they back a ban federal law already has, so they state no rung 3 act (and are out of cycle). His HB 1077 (2022) co-sponsorship has no instrument file and cannot seat. His 2022 words for permit-free carry ("without having to wait weeks to receive a state-mandated license") match rung 5 but date from an earlier term, so they are not relied on.$r4$),
  ('d7ba231a-248d-40d0-a322-a2ffcd26dc04', 4,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00" and HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow", co-sponsor of HB 4943 (2023), the Capitol Commission shall not "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying "in the State Capitol Building": an easier permit, a wider class of carriers and carry in a public building, rung 4, with the permit kept. Single-subject bills on split House votes. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128) and on SB 22, which shows a side only; no record repeals a permit: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4284 (2025), the concealed pistol licence renewal fee cut from "$115.00" to "$30.00", and co-sponsor of HB 4943 (2023), the Capitol Commission shall not "prevent" … "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying "in the State Capitol Building": an easier permit and carry in a public building, rung 4, with the permit kept. Yea on HB 5113 (2025), after an application error the applicant "may submit a new application" … "without paying an additional fee", Yea and co-sponsor on HB 4980 (2025), an applicant may apply to a county clerk in any county in this state and HB 4855 (2025), the act does not prohibit an individual from transporting a pistol or carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not, if any of the following apply: … "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow": a free re-application, applying in any county and a pistol while bow hunting, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. Single-subject bills on split House votes. He voted No on the rung 3 bills (HB 4138, 4146, SB 83, HB 4127, 4128) and on SB 22, which shows a side only; no record repeals a permit: rung 4, make carrying a gun easier.$r4$),
  ('d7dda604-87e2-4613-8d13-3bf2c9f0e6fb', 4,
   $r4$Every counted loosening act keeps the permit and makes carrying easier: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (carry allowed where it was barred, permit system kept: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (applying at a nearer office, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (an easier permit, a fee waiver, annex rule two: rung 4); and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). His No votes on the 2023 restrictions and on SB 22 show a direction only; HB 4481 is gated. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$,
   $r4$Every loosening act keeps the permit: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (Yea and co-sponsor), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (Yea and co-sponsor), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (a free re-application), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; and co-sponsorship of HB 4943 (2023) bars the Capitol Commission from any rule to prevent "An individual licensed by this state or another state to carry a concealed pistol" from carrying in the Capitol (carry in more places for licence holders: rung 4; no floor vote on file, so the sponsorship is not gated). His No votes on the 2023 restrictions and on SB 22 show a direction only; HB 4481 is gated. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$),
  ('d9ca3eac-f51d-4d1a-b7ae-df5249459d24', 5,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". Her Yea on HB 1084 (2024) is a rung 4 act on the same side, so the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by a 2019 bill and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Her SB 158 (2023) authorship is a multi-subject bill. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". Her Yea on HB 1084 (2024) gives a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by a 2019 bill and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Her SB 158 (2023) authorship is a multi-subject bill. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$),
  ('dfab8491-551f-4a3f-b76c-d220a9f4b23b', 5,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Same-side acts: author and Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" widens where a gun may be carried while the permit system stays (rung 4); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; author and Yea on SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" (rung 4); Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays (rung 4). All rung acts are on the loosening side, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His SB 119 (2019) authorship is set aside under that ruling; HB 1095 (2017, armor-piercing ammunition) states no firearm or magazine type. His SEA 176 (2026) words are about local zoning of shooting ranges (adjacent); his 2015 sawed-off-shotgun quote is out of cycle and a type-ban repeal (annex Q4); the campaign-site text is third person.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the Senate conference report of HB 1296 (2022), single-subject, repeals the licence to carry a handgun: the offence of "section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)" is listed as repealed, and it is replaced by a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun" (part of the rung 5 act, closure C3). A state law that removes its own carry permit meets "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry" (furthest act; it also widens carry) (30-20, 2022-03-08, gate none). Other loosening findings: author and Yea on HB 1084 (2024), single-subject: "A state officer who is not prohibited from possessing a handgun under state or federal law has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building" gives a capitol carry right for state officials, which is out of scope (not a rung act); its registry ban repeals no permit or registration requirement; author and Yea on SB 43 (2017), single-subject: a member of the general assembly with a licence "has the right to carry a handgun within the state capitol building", a capitol carry right for lawmakers, which is out of scope (not a rung act); Yea on HB 1071 (2017), single-subject: a person protected by a protection order "may carry a handgun without a license for a period ending sixty (60) days" while the licence system stays, a carry change for one narrow situation, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act. The only rung act left is the rung 5 act, so furthest act gives rung 5. Under the Indiana rulings of 2026-10-31, SB 119 (2019) and SB 344 (2017) copy bans federal law already has and are not rung 3 acts, HB 1365 (2023) is no rung act, sponsorships of HB 1296 (2022) are of the insurance bill as filed and are not a gun act, and sponsorships of multi-subject bills (SB 158 2023, SB 335 2020, HB 1284 2019) show a direction only; none of them makes the record two-sided. Gated votes (near-unanimous, multi-subject) and police-only carry bills are set aside. His SB 119 (2019) authorship is set aside under that ruling; HB 1095 (2017, armor-piercing ammunition) states no firearm or magazine type. His SEA 176 (2026) words are about local zoning of shooting ranges (adjacent); his 2015 sawed-off-shotgun quote is out of cycle and a type-ban repeal (annex Q4); the campaign-site text is third person.$r4$),
  ('e136cc8e-2866-4d10-b94d-c56d345da2cc', 3,
   $r4$State House record. Rung 3 acts: Yea on HB 4138 (2023, PA 19): a person shall not "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license" (checking every buyer); Yea on SB 83 (2023), under which "the restrained individual shall not purchase or possess a firearm", and HB 4146 (2023), which bars a person under an extreme risk protection order from a purchase licence (red-flag orders); and Yea on HB 4127 (2023), a person shall not "While the polls are open on an election day, possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance", and HB 4128 (2023), no firearm in "an absent voter counting place" (bans on guns in named places). All single-subject, none near-unanimous (56-53, 56-51, 56-52, 56-53, 56-53). Rung 4 acts that keep the permit: HB 4855 (2025), carrying "a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (90-13); HB 5113 (2025), a new application after an application error "without paying an additional fee" (78-27). Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05, a permit-keeping easing (a fee cut, an any-county application, a free re-application, a narrow carry widening such as a pistol while bow hunting, lifting a carry ban in an emergency) conflicts neither with the who-may-have limits nor with a ban on carrying in named places, so the record sits at rung 3: "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders". No type ban (rung 2) or permit repeal (rung 5) is in the record. Storage (SB 79, HB 5450), seized-gun disposal (HB 6145/6146) and range liability (HB 4949) are adjacent; SB 22 and SB 471 copy federal bans; HB 4481 is gated near-unanimous (92-9). He co-sponsored HB 4127, HB 4128 and HB 4146. He voted No on HB 4284 and HB 4980 (direction only). The branch.vote profile line is unattributed text (V1) and is not used.$r4$,
