  ('e40b492e-dc22-4150-9c07-6966e01f41a6', 5,
   $r4$Yea on HB 4416 (2017, House, 59-49, single-subject), which deletes from MCL 750.227 the bar on carrying a pistol concealed without a license to carry the pistol as provided by law, leaving the ban only for "A person who is prohibited by state or federal law from possessing a firearm": it repeals the permit to carry (rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). Same legislature, so in-term (V5). His later acts are on the same side and contradict nothing: HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (carry allowed where it was barred, permit system kept: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (applying at a nearer office, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (an easier permit, a fee waiver, annex rule two: rung 4); SB 11 (2021) keeps county clerks' duty to issue concealed pistol licences during a declared emergency (permit kept, easier to get: rung 4) (Senate Yea) — rung 4; furthest act is rung 5. His No votes on the 2023 restrictions show a direction only; HB 4474 and SB 219 (2017) are gated near-unanimous; HB 4285 (school safety course) has no file and is adjacent; his 2018 words on armed school staff are adjacent and out of cycle. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner.$r4$,
