   $r4$As State Representative he co-sponsored and voted Yea on MI HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13, gate none), allowing "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow", and voted Yea on HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45; apply to a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4284 (CPL renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00") and HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"). Each keeps the permit and widens carry or makes the permit easier: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4; annex rule two). No act repeals a permit and none is a rung 1-3 act. HB 4481 is gated (92-9); HB 4949 is adjacent.$r4$,
   $r4$As State Representative he co-sponsored and voted Yea on MI HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13, gate none), allowing "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow", and voted Yea on HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45; apply to a county clerk in any county in this state), HB 4284 (CPL renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00") and HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"). Each keeps the permit. HB 4284 makes the permit easier to get: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4; annex rule two). HB 4855, HB 4980 and HB 5113, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. No act repeals a permit and none is a rung 1-3 act. HB 4481 is gated (92-9); HB 4949 is adjacent.$r4$),
  ('ead3d0f1-4f21-478a-835e-d2118eb5b0c5', 4,
   $r4$Every counted loosening act keeps the permit and makes carrying easier: prime sponsor and Yea on HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 4030 (2021) cuts the CPL application fee from $100.00 to "not more than $45.00" (a lower fee: rung 4); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (carry allowed where it was barred, permit system kept: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (applying at a nearer office, annex rule two: rung 4) (Yea and co-sponsor); HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (an easier permit, a fee waiver, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order from acting to "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm (lifting a carry ban during a declared emergency: rung 4) (Yea and prime sponsor); SB 11 (2021) keeps county clerks' duty to issue concealed pistol licences during a declared emergency (permit kept, easier to get: rung 4). His campaign-site line that "red flag laws in this state must be overturned" (undated; the page belongs to his live 2026 race) is a repeal of a limit that is not a permit (annex Q4): direction only, and it does not contradict rung 4. His 2020 words on fees agree. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$,
   $r4$Every loosening act keeps the permit: prime sponsor and Yea on HB 4284 (2025) cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from $115.00 to "$30.00" (a lower fee, annex rule two: rung 4); HB 4030 (2021) cuts the CPL application fee from $100.00 to "not more than $45.00" (a lower fee: rung 4); HB 4855 (2025) lets an individual carry a loaded pistol while "hunting game with a bow and arrow" (Yea and co-sponsor), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; HB 4980 (2025) lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state instead of the county of residence (Yea and co-sponsor), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; HB 5113 (2025) lets a CPL applicant resubmit a corrected application "without paying an additional fee" (a free re-application), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; HB 5187 (2021) bars an emergency order from acting to "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm (Yea and prime sponsor), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act; SB 11 (2021) keeps county clerks' duty to issue concealed pistol licences during a declared emergency, which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on and is not a rung act. The rung 4 acts are the fee cuts HB 4284 and HB 4030. His campaign-site line that "red flag laws in this state must be overturned" (undated; the page belongs to his live 2026 race) is a repeal of a limit that is not a permit (annex Q4): direction only, and it does not contradict rung 4. His 2020 words on fees agree. SB 22 (2023, airport sterile areas) restates a ban federal law already has and is read as no rung act; if it were read as a rung 3 place ban this record would turn two-sided, so this reading is flagged for the runner. Seats rung 4, "Make carrying a gun easier".$r4$),
  ('eae88f55-a772-4aa7-9bae-8bc4e6c55ced', 5,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". His Yeas on HB 1071 (2017), SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by SB 119 (2019) and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on HB 1296 (2022), conference report (the enrolled text): it repeals the licence to carry ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and sets who may not carry on permit-free carry ("the following persons may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"), which is part of the rung 5 act. That meets "Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry". His Yea on HB 1071 (2017), a carry change for one narrow situation, since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on; his Yeas on SB 43 (2017) and HB 1084 (2024) give a capitol carry right for lawmakers and state officials, which is out of scope. None of the three is a rung act, so the furthest act is 5. The Yea on HB 1365 (2023) is not read as a rung act: its stored text restates the under-18 machine-gun transfer ban already enacted by SB 119 (2019) and amends the machine-gun definition and its exemption list with no deletion markup, so whether it widens or narrows the ban cannot be read; it neither seats nor makes the record two-sided. Other ungated votes (HB 1143, HB 1235, HB 1274, HB 1323 and SB 176 where present) are adjacent: police-agency disposal, suits against gun makers, insurance notices, licence-record privacy and shooting-range zoning. Gated votes (near-unanimous or multi-subject) cannot seat.$r4$),
  ('eb5bada0-58ea-4eaf-90f4-af16bee160f3', 4,
   $r4$As a sitting State Representative he voted Yea on, and co-sponsored, MI HB 4284 (2026-01-14, 63-46, gate none), which cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from "$115.00" to "$30.00", and HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45), which lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; he was prime sponsor of and voted Yea on HB 5187 (2022-03-10, 61-40), under which an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm. With his Yeas on SB 11 (CPL issuance continues during an emergency), HB 4030 (CPL fee to "not more than $45.00"), HB 4855 (bow hunters may carry a loaded pistol) and HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"), these keep the permit and make carrying easier, meeting 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (annex rule two, licence rule list). No act in the findings repeals a permit (rung 5) and he has no Yea on a rung 1-3 act: his Nays on HB 4138, HB 4127, HB 4128, HB 4146, SB 83 and SB 22 show a side only. HB 4481 is gated (92-9); HB 4003, HB 4949 are adjacent. His 2024 voter-guide line 'The right to keep and bear arms shall not be infringed' shows a side only.$r4$,
   $r4$As a sitting State Representative he voted Yea on, and co-sponsored, MI HB 4284 (2026-01-14, 63-46, gate none), which cuts the concealed pistol licence renewal fee from "$115.00" to "$30.00", and HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45), which lets an applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state; he was prime sponsor of and voted Yea on HB 5187 (2022-03-10, 61-40), under which an emergency order "shall not" … "Prohibit, regulate, or curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm. He also voted Yea on SB 11 (CPL issuance continues during an emergency), HB 4030 (CPL fee to "not more than $45.00"), HB 4855 (bow hunters may carry a loaded pistol) and HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"). HB 4980, HB 5187, SB 11, HB 4855 and HB 5113, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. The fee cuts HB 4284 and HB 4030 keep the permit and make it easier to get, meeting 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (annex rule two, licence rule list). No act in the findings repeals a permit (rung 5) and he has no Yea on a rung 1-3 act: his Nays on HB 4138, HB 4127, HB 4128, HB 4146, SB 83 and SB 22 show a side only. HB 4481 is gated (92-9); HB 4003, HB 4949 are adjacent. His 2024 voter-guide line 'The right to keep and bear arms shall not be infringed' shows a side only.$r4$),
  ('f266e021-f787-41b0-8755-09517d77b872', 4,
   $r4$As State Representative he co-sponsored and voted Yea on MI HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45, gate none), which lets a CPL applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, and HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13), which allows "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow"; he voted Yea on HB 4284 (renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00"), HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"), HB 4030, SB 11 and HB 5187 (an emergency order "shall not" … "curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm). These keep the permit and make carrying easier: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4; annex rule two). No act repeals a permit. His only Yea on a restriction bill, SB 22 (2023, firearms barred in an airport's sterile area), copies a ban federal law already has and is no rung act; his Nays on HB 4138, HB 4127, HB 4128, HB 4146 and SB 471 show a side only. HB 4481 is gated (92-9).$r4$,
   $r4$As State Representative he co-sponsored and voted Yea on MI HB 4980 (2026-01-21, 60-45, gate none), which lets a CPL applicant apply to a county clerk in any county in this state, and HB 4855 (2026-02-05, 90-13), which allows "carrying a loaded pistol, whether concealed or not" while "hunting game with a bow and arrow"; he voted Yea on HB 4284 (renewal fee "$115.00" to "$30.00"), HB 5113 (reapply "without paying an additional fee"), HB 4030, SB 11 and HB 5187 (an emergency order "shall not" … "curtail the otherwise lawful possession, carrying" of a firearm). All keep the permit. The fee bills HB 4284 and HB 4030 make the permit easier to get: 'Make carrying a gun easier, such as honoring carry permits from other states' (rung 4; annex rule two). HB 4980, HB 4855, HB 5113, SB 11 and HB 5187, since the 2026-11-12 ruling, show only which side he is on and are not rung acts. No act repeals a permit. His only Yea on a restriction bill, SB 22 (2023, firearms barred in an airport's sterile area), copies a ban federal law already has and is no rung act; his Nays on HB 4138, HB 4127, HB 4128, HB 4146 and SB 471 show a side only. HB 4481 is gated (92-9).$r4$),
  ('f54d43d3-ac9d-4563-a13c-8723f11918ef', 3,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on the rung 4 act HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"), each keeping the permit. Under the licence rule as ruled 2026-11-05 (runner, Michigan), a place ban and a permit-keeping easing do not conflict, and the rung 4 acts sit beside the rung 3 acts that limit who may buy or keep a gun (annex the licence rule); the record sits at rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She co-sponsored HB 4146, HB 4127 and HB 4128. Her Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated; no own words were found.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea on the rung 3 acts HB 4138 (2023, 56-53: no one may "Purchase a firearm that is not a pistol in this state without first having obtained a license"); SB 83 (2023, 56-51: "the court shall issue an extreme risk protection order" on the stated finding); HB 4146 (2023, 56-52: a buyer qualifies only if "not subject to an extreme risk protection order"), and on the place bans HB 4127 (2023, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in a polling place or within 100 feet from any entrance"); HB 4128 (2024, 56-53: no one may "possess a firearm in an absent voter counting place" while ballots are processed). Yea also on HB 4855 (2026, 90-13: carrying a loaded pistol is allowed when "The individual is hunting game with a bow and arrow"), which since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on and is not a rung act; it conflicts with nothing, so the record sits at rung 3 on its rung 3 acts, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods or bans on guns in hospitals". It has no Yea on a type ban or a permit repeal. She co-sponsored HB 4146, HB 4127 and HB 4128. Her Nays on other carry bills show a side only; HB 4481 is gated; no own words were found.$r4$),
  ('f84421ea-eb49-44c3-a6a7-a3cfaf5dc745', 5,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of IN HB 1296 (2022) and Yea on its House conference report, 2022-03-08. HB 1296 (2022) repeals the licence offence ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and lists who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; it removes the permit to carry for adults, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Its list of persons barred from permit-free carry is the condition that the permit-removal law sets on its own permit-free carry, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3), not a separate rung 3 act. Single-subject verdict (season 3, codebook 0.6), census gate none. Her own words back it: background checks happen at purchase and again when they want to carry it ... There's no need for this duplication and the financial burden it imposes upon lawful citizens (caucus release, 2021-03-17, on the bill to let adults carry without a licence). She also co-sponsored HB 1071 (2017) and voted for HB 1084 (2024), rung 4 acts; furthest act is 5. The caucus biography is third-person and not used. No restriction-side rung act is ungated. A Yea on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note); if it were read as rung 2, this record would become two-sided and the row a BLANK adjacent-chairs.$r4$,
   $r4$Co-sponsor of IN HB 1296 (2022) and Yea on its House conference report, 2022-03-08. HB 1296 (2022) repeals the licence offence ("section 1 of this chapter (carrying a handgun without a license) (before its repeal)") and lists who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun"; it removes the permit to carry for adults, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Its list of persons barred from permit-free carry is the condition that the permit-removal law sets on its own permit-free carry, part of the rung 5 act (annex closure C3), not a separate rung 3 act. Single-subject verdict (season 3, codebook 0.6), census gate none. Her own words back it: background checks happen at purchase and again when they want to carry it ... There's no need for this duplication and the financial burden it imposes upon lawful citizens (caucus release, 2021-03-17, on the bill to let adults carry without a licence). She also co-sponsored HB 1071 (2017), a carry change for one narrow situation that since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side she is on, and voted for HB 1084 (2024), a capitol carry right for state officials that is out of scope; neither is a rung act, so the furthest act is 5. The caucus biography is third-person and not used. No restriction-side rung act is ungated. A Yea on HB 1365 (2023, machine guns) is treated as no rung act (orchestrator note); if it were read as rung 2, this record would become two-sided and the row a BLANK adjacent-chairs.$r4$),
  ('fb5368e7-35c6-4ae0-b97b-b2e1fb5fb9a6', 5,
   $r4$Yea in the House on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Yeas on HB 1071 (2017) and SB 43 (2017) (rung 4) sit below it under furthest act. His 2011 press-release words on a 2011 bill are out of cycle and not relied on; the HB 1296 co-sponsorship is evidence of the bill as filed and is not cited.$r4$,
   $r4$Yea in the House on the HB 1296 (2022) conference committee report (the rung 5 act, runner ruling): Sec. 10 repeals the licence requirement "Section 1 of this chapter does not apply to" and Sec. 9 replaces it with a list of persons who "may not knowingly or intentionally carry a handgun", so an adult not on that list may carry a handgun with no licence. That is a law that repeals the permit to carry (rung 5: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry"). Single-subject (season 3 verdict), ungated. His Yea on HB 1071 (2017), a carry change for one narrow situation, since the 2026-11-12 ruling shows only which side he is on; his Yea on SB 43 (2017), a capitol carry right for lawmakers, is out of scope. Neither is a rung act. His 2011 press-release words on a 2011 bill are out of cycle and not relied on; the HB 1296 co-sponsorship is evidence of the bill as filed and is not cited.$r4$),
